WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.602
-Our first guest is
a seven-time Grammy winner

00:00:03.602 --> 00:00:07.173
who has a brand-new
number-one album out now

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.141
called "Happier Than Ever."

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:11.811
Here is Billie Eilish!

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:13.245
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:17.516
Whoa! Come on, now!

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:20.453
Ah, thank you so much
for coming back on the show.

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.055
I appreciate it.

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:24.490
And congrats on having
the number-one album

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.193
in the U.S.
This is big.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.162
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:31.898
-The reviews have been amazing.

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:33.466
I don't know
if you read reviews,

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:36.403
or what do you if --
What have you done?

00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:38.537
The album's been out,
what, a week?

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.172
Have you been paying attention?

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:42.875
Do you go like, "Ah, it's out.
It's my thing."

00:00:42.875 --> 00:00:45.344
-Uh, I don't know, really.

00:00:45.344 --> 00:00:48.180
I'm still trying to figure out
what I'm doing.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.950
I tried to kind of not look.
-Yeah.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.252
-Just because I was --
I was a-scared.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:56.389
And I didn't want to --
-"I was a-scared," yeah.

00:00:56.389 --> 00:01:01.560
-But it's been -- it's been
such a good response that I --

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.731
I'm unbelievably, like,
over-the-moon happy about,

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.432
so it's been pretty crazy
this week.

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:09.569
-I know you're close
to your fans and you --

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:13.873
You love them, and I feel like
they just all went ballistic.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.441
When they're like,
"Yeah. New stuff!"

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:16.809
And they all just came back --

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:19.779
I mean, I've seen the stuff
on the socials.

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.881
I've read the reviews.
And it's just cool.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.382
You have a great fan base,

00:01:23.382 --> 00:01:25.818
and you give them a quality
record, and they love it.

00:01:25.818 --> 00:01:27.620
So congrats.
-Thanks, Jimmy.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.922
-Yeah.
-It's crazy.

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:32.158
-Do you think -- When
you're recording the album,

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:33.425
do you go, "Okay.

00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:35.595
It's gonna be called
'Happier Than Ever.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:36.963
I'm gonna do this.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.531
I'm gonna dye my hair.
I'm gonna go blonde."

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.700
Do you have this planned out
or it all just kind of happen?

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.102
-It really just happened.

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:45.505
I, like, didn't know
what I was going to do at all,

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:49.608
I mean, until, really, like
almost when the album was over.

00:01:49.608 --> 00:01:51.244
I didn't even know
what it was gonna be called.

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:53.413
I didn't know what the vibe
was gonna be.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.281
I didn't know
what the artwork should be.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:58.618
And I knew, though,
that I wanted it to be --

00:01:58.618 --> 00:02:03.022
Like, I wanted it
to feel very specific?

00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:04.523
You know?

00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:08.061
I wanted it to have a real --
you know, feeling to it,

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:10.062
and a real, like,
aesthetic to it, I guess.

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:11.798
And then there was just
one night

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.933
when I was listening
to Julie London,

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:16.236
and it was raining,
and my fire pit was on,

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:19.739
and I was like --
It just, like, hit me.

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.909
And I realized how I wanted
the album to be portrayed

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:25.645
and how I wanted
the visuals to look

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.846
and how I wanted it to feel
and the colors.

00:02:27.846 --> 00:02:30.583
And it all just, like, clicked
right away. I don't know.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.853
-And you direct your own videos
or have done that,

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.521
which is extra pressure.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.690
-Yes.
-Why? Why put that --

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.727
Why put that more pressure
on yourself?

00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:43.229
-Why? [ Laughs ]

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:46.632
Um, you know,
it's a lot of work.

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:48.801
So, you know, sometimes I wish

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:51.170
that I didn't want
to do it so bad.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.974
I just really want to do it.
Like, I just -- There's not --

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:58.510
It never worked for me
before, really.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:02:59.912
I mean, sometimes it does.

00:02:59.912 --> 00:03:02.448
It's just so much --
I don't know.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.817
I feel like
when you have your own idea

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:08.988
and you know what you want,
sometimes it's just the --

00:03:08.988 --> 00:03:11.291
The best way to go about it
is to just do it yourself,

00:03:11.291 --> 00:03:13.693
even though it takes
much more out of you.

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:15.828
-You always bring us
some fun things.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:17.497
So, you have a little
behind-the-scenes look

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.832
at you directing yourself
in this video.

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.568
Take a look.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.378
-First of all...

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.112
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:34.112 --> 00:03:36.115
Don't you think you could have
gotten a stunt double

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:37.783
to stand in the middle there?

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.485
Were you nervous?

00:03:39.485 --> 00:03:41.753
-I -- No.
I really wasn't nervous.

00:03:41.753 --> 00:03:44.524
So that was for "NDA."

00:03:44.524 --> 00:03:50.129
And it was
25 stunt drivers driving,

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.166
zooming past me, like this close
to the sides of me.

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:55.635
And I -- It was so much fun.

00:03:55.635 --> 00:03:58.404
And everybody was scared,
and I loved it.

00:03:58.404 --> 00:03:59.972
I literally, like --
That's my --

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:02.041
That's where I belong,
is, like --

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.610
-In the middle of zooming cars?
-Yeah.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.712
Yeah.
-That would frighten me.

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:08.648
I would be so frightened
if I was anyone --

00:04:08.648 --> 00:04:10.783
Going like, "No.
This is an American treasure.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.718
This is Billie Eilish
standing in front

00:04:12.718 --> 00:04:14.753
of all these zooming cars
going back and forth.

00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:16.189
And then kicking up dust?

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:18.090
That even makes it
even more tricky.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.325
Oh, no, I'm out."

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:20.593
-No.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.461
You know what
I wanted to ask you?

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:25.097
Because I read a bunch
of articles about you,

00:04:25.097 --> 00:04:26.465
and I know that you're very --

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.401
You have a thing.
I don't know what it's called.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:30.203
You're a very colorful person.
You have a --

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:32.071
-Synesthesia.
That's the word you're thinking.

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.372
-Yes. Say it again.

00:04:33.372 --> 00:04:35.007
-Synesthesia.
-Synesthesia.

00:04:35.007 --> 00:04:38.610
So everything you see
kind of has colors to it

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.914
and a number, maybe,
and a letter or something?

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:44.683
-So, synesthesia, just real
quick to sum it up, it's --

00:04:44.683 --> 00:04:47.987
I don't know why it exists.
But my brother has it.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:50.189
I have it. My dad has it.
A few people I know have it.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:52.992
My mom does not.
It's a thing in your brain

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:57.096
where you associate
random stuff to everything.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.965
So for instance,
every day of the week

00:04:59.965 --> 00:05:02.635
has a color, a number, a shape.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.271
Sometimes things have a smell
that I can think of

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:08.340
or a temperature or a texture.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.875
And it means nothing.

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:11.977
I mean, literally,
it doesn't mean anything.

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:14.013
But it inspires
a bunch of stuff.

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.048
So, like, all of my videos

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.751
for the most part
have to do with synesthesia,

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:22.254
all of my artwork, all of my --
everything I do live.

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:23.789
All the colors for each songs

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:27.260
is because those are the colors
for those songs specifically.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.795
And you -- You want to know?
For you?

00:05:29.795 --> 00:05:34.534
-Yes. When you see me
or hear me or smell me...

00:05:34.534 --> 00:05:35.734
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:38.804
...what do you think?

00:05:38.804 --> 00:05:40.573
What is my shape?

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:42.975
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:42.975 --> 00:05:45.711
So, your shape is a...

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:49.949
like, vertical...

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:53.052
brown rectangle.

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:55.120
-A vertical round rectangle.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:58.390
-Yeah, and I don't --
I mean, I can try to explain it.

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.827
-No.

00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:02.628
-It doesn't make any sense.

00:06:02.628 --> 00:06:05.098
So, like, J.
So, your name's Jimmy, right?

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:07.033
-Yep.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.001
-J is brown to me.

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.803
I don't know why.
Jimmy is also brown.

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:13.973
M's feel brown to me.
-M?

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:17.810
-And you also feel like
that color, to me.

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.745
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.745 --> 00:06:21.780
-I feel --
-[ Scattered applause ]

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:25.084
-The audience seems
to half agree that I'm brown.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:27.186
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:30.889
I feel like I'm more of a red.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:33.025
-No.
-[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:35.394
-You're right! You're right!
I'm not gonna argue with you.

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:38.698
-But this is the funny thing
about synesthesia,

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:41.868
is that it's so specific for me.

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:43.635
But, like, for instance,
my dad and my brother

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:45.071
both have synesthesia.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:46.872
They have the same thing
for people,

00:06:46.872 --> 00:06:49.108
and they completely disagree
with me.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:53.045
So we'll have conversations
where my dad will be like --

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.614
You know, I'll be like,

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:57.082
"Oh, Tuesday is purple
and a circle."

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:58.918
My dad is like, "No, it's not.

00:06:58.918 --> 00:07:01.353
It's, you know, orange and
it's a square," or whatever.

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:02.821
And we have these --

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:06.092
We have arguments
about what is what.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:08.060
-These are
the greatest arguments

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:09.194
I've ever heard of in my life.

00:07:09.194 --> 00:07:10.562
-They're very stupid.
Very stupid.

00:07:10.562 --> 00:07:12.564
-Billie, I want to ask you
more stuff about the album

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:14.633
and the tour when we come back.
Stick around, everybody.

00:07:14.633 --> 00:07:16.202
More with Billie Eilish.

